    <section class=3D"u-clearfix u-grey-5 u-section-2" id=3D"sec-a6ff">
      <div class=3D"u-clearfix u-sheet u-sheet-1">
        <h3 class=3D"u-text u-text-1">Step 1</h3>
        <h2 class=3D"u-text u-text-default u-text-2">Download CupCarbon</h2=
>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-3"> Visit th=
e following website and donwload CupCarbon:<br>
          <a href=3D"http://www.cupcarbon.com/" class=3D"u-active-none u-bo=
rder-none u-btn u-button-link u-button-style u-hover-none u-none u-text-pal=
ette-1-base u-btn-1">www.cupcarbon.com</a>
          <br>You unzip the file cupcarbon.zip<br>You will get the followin=
g files:
        </p>
        <img class=3D"u-align-center u-border-1 u-border-grey-75 u-image u-=
image-contain u-image-default u-image-1" src=3D"https://cupcarbon.com/image=
s/Screenshot2023-10-18at10.28.08.png" alt=3D"" data-image-width=3D"221" dat=
a-image-height=3D"108">
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-4">You need =
just to execute the jar file cupcarbon.jar<br>If CupCarbon starts, then you=
 are lucky, you can continue to use it&nbsp;normally<br>Otherwise, it means=
 you have a recent version of Java<br>CupCarbon works only with the version=
 8 (1.8.xxx). Since we are using JavaFX platform, CupCarbon can run only wi=
th the Java 8 version.<br>Therefore, you may install the version 8 (JRE 1.8=
)&nbsp;of java in your system<br>To do that, visit the following web page: =
(<a href=3D"https://cupcarbon.com/www.java.com" class=3D"u-active-none u-bo=
rder-none u-btn u-button-link u-button-style u-hover-none u-none u-text-pal=
ette-1-base u-btn-2">www.java.com</a>). Verify that you are installing the =
version 8.<br>Once done, you can double click again on the file cupcarbon.j=
ar and start using CupCarbon normally.<br>
          <br>If CupCarbon does not start then you may check the version of=
 java installed in your system, you can use the following command:<br>
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-align-center u-custom-font u-font-courier-new u-text =
u-text-custom-color-6 u-text-5">java -version</p>
        <img class=3D"u-image u-image-contain u-image-default u-image-2" sr=
c=3D"https://cupcarbon.com/images/Screenshot2023-10-17at15.37.55.png" alt=
=3D"" data-image-width=3D"593" data-image-height=3D"72">
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-6">Verify th=
at the version is 1.8.xxx<br>In case where the version is not 8 then depend=
ing on your system you should either change the current default version to =
8 or to install the jre directly from the website of oracle:<br>
          <br>
          <a href=3D"https://www.oracle.com/java/technologies/downloads/#ja=
va8-mac" class=3D"u-active-none u-border-none u-btn u-button-link u-button-=
style u-hover-none u-none u-text-palette-1-base u-btn-3">https://www.oracle=
.com/java/technologies/downloads/#java8-mac</a>
          <br>Go to the section:&nbsp;Java SE Development Kit 8u391<br>Choo=
se your platform and download the installer.<br>Once Java installed run Cup=
Carbon using the java command:<br>
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-courier-new u-text u-text-custom-c=
olor-6 u-text-7"> java -jar cupcarbon.jar</p>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-8"> If it do=
es not work, then run CupCarbon using the complete path of Java. For exampl=
e, for mac OS, go to the directory where the cupcarbon.jar is situated and =
write the following command:</p>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-courier-new u-text u-text-custom-c=
olor-6 u-text-9">/Library/Java/JavaVirtualMachines/jdk1.8xx.jdk/Contents/Ho=
me/bin/java -jar cupcarbon.jar</p>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-10">jdk1.8xx=
 can be any version starting with 1.8.<br>
        </p>
        <img class=3D"u-image u-image-contain u-image-default u-image-3" sr=
c=3D"https://cupcarbon.com/images/Screenshot2023-10-19at13.28.38.png" alt=
=3D"" data-image-width=3D"1920" data-image-height=3D"1080">
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class=3D"u-clearfix u-grey-5 u-section-3" id=3D"carousel_004c"=
>
      <div class=3D"u-clearfix u-sheet u-sheet-1">
        <h3 class=3D"u-text u-text-1">Step 2</h3>
        <h2 class=3D"u-text u-text-default u-text-2">Install Python</h2>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-3">You can i=
nstall any version of Python you want.&nbsp;<br>To verify if Python is inst=
alled you can use the following command:
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-align-center u-custom-font u-font-courier-new u-text =
u-text-custom-color-6 u-text-4">python -V</p>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-5"> Python i=
s completely independent from CupCarbon, once installed, you have to use th=
e system command&nbsp; used to execute Python programs.<br>Basically this c=
ommand can by python or python3<br>
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-align-center u-custom-font u-font-courier-new u-text =
u-text-custom-color-6 u-text-6">python<br>
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-7">It can be=
 a complete path where the command is situated in your system. You can add =
this commande (or path) in the Executor Path Configuration window as shown =
by the following figures:<br>
        </p>
        <img class=3D"u-align-center u-image u-image-contain u-image-defaul=
t u-image-1" src=3D"https://cupcarbon.com/images/executore_py.png" alt=3D""=
 data-image-width=3D"560" data-image-height=3D"291">
        <p class=3D"u-align-center u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-te=
xt-custom-color-2 u-text-8">Now you can use CupCarbon, enjoy!</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class=3D"u-clearfix u-grey-5 u-section-4" id=3D"carousel_9c69"=
>
      <div class=3D"u-clearfix u-sheet u-sheet-1">
        <h3 class=3D"u-text u-text-1">Step 3</h3>
        <h2 class=3D"u-text u-text-default u-text-2">Visualisation</h2>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-3">To intera=
ce with CupCarbon from your Python codes, you can send commands to CupCarbo=
n and you can get commands from CupCarbon as well.<br>To send commands from=
 your code Python to CupCarbon, use the command print of Python normally.&n=
bsp; The message of the print will be considered as a CupCarbon command. Yo=
u need to flush each command to send it directly, otherwise, all the comman=
ds will be sent once at the end of the program. In this case you have to us=
e the command:<br>sys.stdout.flush() after each print command<br>
          <br>To use this command you need to import the library sys, as fo=
llows:&nbsp;<br>import sys<br>
          <br>In a nutshell:<br>Any Python program communicating with CupCa=
rbon has to import the library sys, to send the CupCarbon comands using the=
 command print and to flush the message (send it to CupCarbon) using the co=
mmand flush:<br>
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-align-left u-custom-font u-font-courier-new u-text u-=
text-custom-color-6 u-text-4">print("CupCarbon command", flush=3DTrue)<br>
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-align-left u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text=
-5"> The different commands that you can send to CupCarbon are given as fol=
lows:<br>
          <br>1. print<br>To print any message on the executing device, use=
 the command print as follows:
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-align-left u-custom-font u-font-courier-new u-text u-=
text-custom-color-6 u-text-6">print("print Hellow World!", flush=3DTrue)<br=
>
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-7">2. mark/u=
nmark<br>To mark or unmark the executing device, use the commande mark or u=
nmark as follows:
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-align-left u-custom-font u-font-courier-new u-text u-=
text-custom-color-6 u-text-8">print("mark", flush=3DTrue)<br>print("unmark"=
, flush=3DTrue)<br>
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-9">3. move<b=
r>To move the executing device to a given GPS location, use the move comman=
d as follows:<br>
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-align-left u-custom-font u-font-courier-new u-text u-=
text-custom-color-6 u-text-10">print("move 50.002 21.344", flush=3DTrue)<br=
>
        </p>
        <p class=3D"u-align-left u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text=
-11">4. To get any information about the executing device sugh as, its id, =
its name and its location, use the following commands:</p>
        <p class=3D"u-align-left u-custom-font u-font-courier-new u-text u-=
text-custom-color-6 u-text-12">print("getid", flush=3DTrue)<br>id =3D input=
()<br>
          <br>print("getname", flush=3DTrue)<br>name =3D input()<br>
          <br>print("getxy", flush=3DTrue)<br>location =3D input()<br>
          <br>print("getx", flush=3DTrue)<br>x =3D input()<br>
          <br>print("gety", flush=3DTrue)<br>y =3D input()<br>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class=3D"u-clearfix u-grey-5 u-section-5" id=3D"carousel_b0be"=
>
      <div class=3D"u-clearfix u-sheet u-valign-middle u-sheet-1">
        <h3 class=3D"u-text u-text-1">Step 4</h3>
        <h2 class=3D"u-text u-text-default u-text-2">Install MQTT for Pytho=
n</h2>
        <p class=3D"u-custom-font u-font-georgia u-text u-text-3">To create=
 IoT Projects using the MQTT protocol, just install MQTT for Python.&nbsp;<=
br>You can use the following command:<br>pip install paho-mqtt<br>or<br>pip=
3 install paho-mqtt<br>
          <br>Many tutorials exists on the web. We suggest the following we=
bsites<br>
          <a href=3D"https://pypi.org/project/paho-mqtt/" class=3D"u-active=
-none u-border-none u-btn u-button-link u-button-style u-hover-none u-none =
u-text-palette-1-base u-btn-1">https://pypi.org/project/paho-mqtt/</a>
          <br>
          <a href=3D"https://www.emqx.com/en/blog/how-to-use-mqtt-in-python=
" class=3D"u-active-none u-border-none u-btn u-button-link u-button-style u=
-hover-none u-none u-text-palette-1-base u-btn-2">https://www.emqx.com/en/b=
log/how-to-use-mqtt-in-python<br>
          </a>
          <br>It is possible to use MQTT in different real devices (Android=
 device, iOS, ESP32, Raspberry, ...) to communicate with CupCarbon devices.=
&nbsp;<br>
          <br>You can install Mosquitto to use MQTT from the terminal of yo=
ur system.<br>
          <a href=3D"https://mosquitto.org/download/" class=3D"u-active-non=
e u-border-none u-btn u-button-link u-button-style u-hover-none u-none u-te=
xt-palette-1-base u-btn-3">https://mosquitto.org/download/</a>
          <br>
          <br>Two samples (Pbulisher and Subscriber) are given in CupCarbon=
 that can be used directly. Don't forget to set the value of the broker and=
 the topics if you are using these codes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
